# Architecture Rules - Module Organization & File Structure

Adapted from `devdraft/old-repos/admin-api`'s conventions for this project's module set (auth, onboarding, sessions, wallets, pharmacy). Follow this for every module added while building the phases in [PHASES.md](../../PHASES.md).

## Module Organization

### Feature-Based Modules
Each business domain gets its own top-level module under `src/`:
```
src/
├── auth/              # JWT issuance, guards, OTP verification flow
├── otp/                # OTP request/verify (hardcoded 000000 in DEMO_MODE)
├── rbac/               # Role enum, @Roles() decorator, RolesGuard
├── users/              # Shared User model/resolver (me query)
├── patient/            # Self-onboarding, profile, publicId
├── qr-token/           # Short-lived rotating QR token (Redis-backed)
├── hospital/           # Hospital onboarding, consultationFee, hospital admin views
├── doctor/             # Doctor onboarding (by hospital admin)
├── session/            # Session lifecycle: start/stop, vitals, diagnosis, prescription
├── pharmacy/           # Pharmacy onboarding, drug price list, dispense flow
├── wallet/             # WalletProvider interface + MockWalletProvider, ledger
├── notification/       # GraphQL subscription publishers (Redis pub/sub backed)
├── prisma/             # PrismaService + PrismaModule
├── graphql/            # GraphQL module wiring, shared scalars/types
└── common/             # Shared utilities, guards, filters, redis client
```

### Standard Module Imports
All feature modules import these core modules as needed:
```typescript
@Module({
  imports: [
    PrismaModule,                   // Database access
    AuthModule,                     // Authentication (only if guarding own resolvers)
    forwardRef(() => CommonModule), // Shared services (redis, etc.)
  ],
  providers: [FeatureService, FeatureResolver],
  exports: [FeatureService],
})
```

### Circular Dependencies
Use `forwardRef()` for circular imports:
```typescript
// In module A that needs module B
imports: [forwardRef(() => ModuleBModule)]

// In module B that needs module A
imports: [forwardRef(() => ModuleAModule)]
```
Expected circular pair in this project: `session` ↔ `wallet` (finalize charges a wallet; wallet ledger references session as `referenceId`) and `pharmacy` ↔ `wallet` (dispense charges a wallet). Resolve with `forwardRef()`, don't merge the modules.

## File Organization

### Naming Conventions
- **Files/Directories**: kebab-case (`qr-token`, `prescription-item`)
- **Classes**: PascalCase (`SessionService`, `WalletResolver`)
- **Variables/Functions**: camelCase (`startSession`, `getWalletBalance`)

### File Suffixes
- Services: `.service.ts`
- Resolvers: `.resolver.ts`
- Controllers: `.controller.ts` (only if a REST endpoint is truly needed — this project is GraphQL-first, prefer resolvers)
- DTOs / GraphQL inputs: `.dto.ts` (holds both `@InputType()` input classes and plain DTOs)
- Models: `.model.ts` (GraphQL `@ObjectType()` classes)
- Modules: `.module.ts`
- Guards: `.guard.ts`
- Enums: `.enum.ts`
- Interfaces: `.interface.ts` (e.g. `wallet-provider.interface.ts`)

### Directory Structure per Feature
```
feature-name/
├── feature-name.module.ts           # Module definition
├── feature-name.service.ts          # Business logic
├── feature-name.resolver.ts         # GraphQL resolver (queries + mutations)
├── dto/                              # GraphQL inputs
│   └── create-feature.dto.ts
├── models/                           # GraphQL object types
│   └── feature.model.ts
├── enums/                             # Feature-specific enums (mirror Prisma enums)
│   └── feature-status.enum.ts
└── interfaces/                       # Provider abstractions (e.g. wallet/wallet-provider.interface.ts)
```
Only add `dto/`, `models/`, `enums/`, `interfaces/` subfolders once a feature has more than one or two files of that kind — a module with a single DTO can keep `feature-name.dto.ts` flat at the module root (see `wallet.dto.ts`-style flattening in the reference repo for small modules).

### Import Order
```typescript
// 1. Node modules
import { Injectable, ForbiddenException } from '@nestjs/common';
import { Args, Mutation, Resolver } from '@nestjs/graphql';

// 2. Internal modules (services, common)
import { PrismaService } from '../prisma/prisma.service';
import { RedisService } from '../common/redis/redis.service';

// 3. Types and interfaces
import { Session as PrismaSession } from '@prisma/client';
import { StartSessionInput } from './dto/start-session.dto';

// 4. Local imports
import { SessionModel } from './models/session.model';
import { SessionStatus } from './enums/session-status.enum';
```

## Module Composition Patterns

### Core Infrastructure Modules
- `PrismaModule` — Database access (Postgres)
- `AuthModule` — JWT guards, current-user decorator
- `RbacModule` — `@Roles()` decorator + `RolesGuard`
- `CommonModule` — Redis client, shared error filters
- `NotificationModule` — subscription publish helpers, imported by any module that needs to emit a realtime event

### Feature Module Template
```typescript
import { Module, forwardRef } from '@nestjs/common';
import { PrismaModule } from '../prisma/prisma.module';
import { AuthModule } from '../auth/auth.module';
import { CommonModule } from '../common/common.module';

import { FeatureService } from './feature.service';
import { FeatureResolver } from './feature.resolver';

@Module({
  imports: [
    PrismaModule,
    AuthModule,
    forwardRef(() => CommonModule),
  ],
  providers: [FeatureService, FeatureResolver],
  exports: [FeatureService],
})
export class FeatureModule {}
```

## GraphQL Conventions
- Code-first schema (`@nestjs/graphql` + `@nestjs/apollo`), auto-generated SDL written to `src/schema.gql` — this generated file must match [schema.graphql](../../schema.graphql) at the repo root, which is the source-of-truth contract shared with frontend. If they diverge, fix the resolver/model, not the contract file.
- One resolver per feature module; don't scatter a feature's queries/mutations across multiple resolver files unless the feature is large enough to warrant splitting (e.g. `session.resolver.ts` vs a future `session-vitals.resolver.ts` only if it grows unwieldy).
- Role guards applied via `@UseGuards(GqlAuthGuard, RolesGuard)` + `@Roles(Role.DOCTOR)` at the resolver method level, not globally — different fields on the same resolver can have different role requirements (e.g. `Query.me` is open to any authenticated role).

## Environment & Configuration

### Environment Files
- `.env` — local development
- `.env.example` — committed template, no real secrets
- `.env.test` — test runs

### Configuration Pattern
```typescript
@Injectable()
export class SomeService {
  constructor(private configService: ConfigService) {}

  private getQrTokenTtlSeconds(): number {
    return this.configService.get<number>('QR_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS', 120);
  }
}
```
`DEMO_MODE=true` gates the hardcoded-OTP behavior and the `MockWalletProvider` — keep both switches named and visible in `.env.example` so they're obvious to disable before any real deployment.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

### ❌ Don't
- Mix different business domains in one module (e.g. don't fold `pharmacy` pricing logic into `wallet`)
- Create circular dependencies without `forwardRef()`
- Put business logic (wallet debits, token validation) directly in resolvers — resolvers call services
- Reintroduce a static/public patient lookup field — the QR token is deliberately short-lived and Redis-backed, not a Prisma column
- Hand-edit the generated `src/schema.gql` — change the resolver/model and regenerate

### ✅ Do
- Keep modules focused on a single business domain from the PRD's module list
- Mirror Prisma enums with GraphQL enums one-to-one (`SessionStatus`, `PrescriptionItemStatus`, etc.)
- Emit notification events from the service layer right after the state change that causes them (see PRD §5.5's event table for the mapping)
- Export only the service (not the resolver) from each module, so other modules can depend on business logic without pulling in GraphQL wiring

